1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
I'm Terry Gerard. I'm Scoutmaster with Group 44 in Collinsville, Oklahoma.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
This edition of the Scoutmaster podcast is sponsored by backers like me.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:13,000
And now to you, Scoutmaster.

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:24,000
I heard a cheer go up from one of our patrol campsites on our camp out last weekend

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,000
and I was curious to know what all the excitement was about.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,000
so I walked over and I asked and the patrol leader told me

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:36,000
that they had finished a jigsaw puzzle that they brought along in just two hours.

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,000
And I said: wow, that's really something good for you.

9
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:44,000
And the patrol leader said yeah, the box said four to six years.

10
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,000
This is podcast number 213.. Hey,

11
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Whoa,

12
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster podcast. This is Clark Green.

13
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:23,000
John wrote in to say you've been a huge help to me as I get back into the thick of things in scouting.

14
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,000
You provide a great outside opinion on what I see and don't understand in my troop.

15
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,000
Well, John, I'm glad to be of help. Tom wrote in and said: great show this past week.

16
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:37,000
I enjoyed podcast 212.. I got a lot out of that episode.

17
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:42,000
There were great specifics and I really like your advice on dealing with new scout parents and leaders.

18
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Setting the right tone and expectations from the get-go is best

19
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:50,000
A Scoutmaster- or an advisor in my case- needs to be a mentor to youth

20
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:52,000
and a leader to other scout leaders and parents.

21
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:57,000
It can be hard to switch gears, but keeping focused on the goal at hand helps.

22
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Keep up the good work. See you around the campfire. Thanks, Tom,

23
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,000
I'm glad you got something out of that.

24
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Andy McDonald did too. He's with Scout Troop 196 in Winnersprings, Florida.

25
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:11,000
Thank you, as always, for a great podcast.

26
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:15,000
As the new Scoutmaster, I've been making changes to our troop in the past four months

27
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,000
and this podcast reinforced a number of those that I have implemented.

28
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,000
One of them was that I don't allow adults to make announcements

29
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:28,000
or to address the troop during meetings unless it's a special occasion like a court of honor.

30
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,000
I ask that they bring announcements to me and I hand them to the senior patrol leader.

31
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Another change is having only registered and trained leaders on camp out.

32
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:39,000
I've had a little bit of backlash on some of the things.

33
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:43,000
However, most of the changes have been well received by those that understand

34
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,000
how a troop's supposed to be run.

35
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:50,000
This podcast gave me another booster shot to keep me going on the same track I've been thus far.

36
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Thank you immensely for everything you do for us.

37
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Thank you, Andy, for getting in touch,

38
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,000
And I'm really happy to hear that you're finding things helpful.

39
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:03,000
Listen. if you are a regular reader or listener and, like Andy and Tom and John,

40
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,000
you're finding what's offered by ScoutmasterCGcom has helped you,

41
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:11,000
You can return the favor by becoming a ScoutmasterCGcom backer.

42
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:15,000
Funds from backers go towards the expenses of producing and publishing the blog

43
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,000
and the posts and the podcast,

44
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:21,000
And they keep these resources freely accessible to Scouters all over the world.

45
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:27,000
And this past week I want to thank Terry Gerard and John Strohmeier

46
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,000
who stepped up and helped out.

47
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,000
Thank you so much, guys.

48
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:33,000
I really do appreciate it.

49
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,000
Well, in this week's podcast in ScoutmasterCG, in seven minutes or less,

50
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:41,000
we're going to talk about the tremendous, powerful potential of our attitude

51
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:45,000
when we're dealing with Scouts in that developmental stage of life

52
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,000
that we all know so very well, right?

53
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,000
And then we've got an email question to answer,

54
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,000
So that's going to take up the rest of the podcast.

55
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Let's get started, shall we?

56
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:59,000
Well, I know this probably doesn't happen to you,

57
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:03,000
but sometimes my Scouts act badly,

58
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:07,000
I mean just objectively poorly.

59
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Sometimes they're stubborn,

60
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,000
They're lazy,

61
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:13,000
They're indifferent,

62
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,000
They're short-sighted

63
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,000
And they do foolish things.

64
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Now, why is that happening

65
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
And what am I supposed to do about it as a Scouter.

66
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:25,000
Wouldn't it be great if I could spend the next few minutes

67
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:28,000
and tell you exactly how to fix all that?

68
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,000
I'm not going to be able to,

69
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:34,000
But I'm going to be able to share with you some thoughts

70
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:38,000
about how to work with Scouts in a way that's going to make you

71
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:40,000
happier and make them happier.

72
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:42,000
And well, how do I do that

73
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Scouts in a way that's going to make you happier

74
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,000
and make them happier and will help you better achieve

75
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,000
the aims of Scouting.

76
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,000
So why do Scouts act that way?

77
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Why are they that way sometimes?

78
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,000
Well, there's a two-part answer.

79
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,000
One is is that they're human beings

80
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,000
and we're all prone to doing that every once in a while,

81
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:04,000
no matter what our age,

82
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
And the other one is because they're progressing

83
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,000
through a developmental stage of life

84
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:11,000
that isn't always pretty,

85
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:15,000
But it's rather predictable and it's very normal and healthy.

86
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:19,000
I think one of the more useful things I could share with you

87
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:22,000
is the idea that, to make real progress

88
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,000
and to be useful to our Scouts,

89
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:28,000
I think there's one key shift in our thinking as adults

90
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,000
that's going to help us do that,

91
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:33,000
And that is that outside influences are not causing

92
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:35,000
these problems.

93
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,000
Now, are there objectively bad influences

94
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
in the world out there,

95
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,000
Of course,

96
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,000
But are these bad influences

97
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:48,000
a significant cause of the difficult behavior

98
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:50,000
and attitudes we see in our Scouts?

99
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:52,000
I really don't think so,

100
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,000
And let me explain why.

101
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,000
I think it's all too easy for us to blame

102
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:59,000
these what I would call adolescent instabilities

103
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:02,000
and uncertainties on outside influences

104
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:05,000
like- oh I don't know- too much government

105
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:08,000
or not enough government or society at large,

106
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:12,000
or general moral decay and shifts in culture.

107
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:15,000
There's just one fundamental problem

108
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:17,000
with looking at the world that way:

109
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,000
If we look at the last century

110
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,000
and we look at all of the moral panics

111
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
that we're supposed to end civilization as we know it,

112
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,000
All you have to do is go back and look at old newspapers.

113
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
I mean votes for women.

114
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:35,000
We're going to end the world dancing in public.

115
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:38,000
We're going to end the world with newspapers.

116
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
They were horrible. novels-

117
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,000
Oh my gosh. movies,

118
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,000
Oh no. radio.

119
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
Radio coming into the home,

120
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,000
Television, the internet, comic books, rock music-

121
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:52,000
You could just the list is endless. isn't it

122
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:55,000
A pervasive and broadly accepted formula

123
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:58,000
that we've all used? is that the things

124
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,000
that we see in our Scouts-

125
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:04,000
the whole list, stubbornness

126
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:06,000
and shortsightedness and foolishness-

127
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:09,000
is caused by some fundamental change in the world

128
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:12,000
since you and I were kids.

129
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:15,000
Now our parents and their parents,

130
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:17,000
and the parents of their parents

131
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:21,000
all the way back- they in some respects

132
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:24,000
thought that the world was headed to hell

133
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:25,000
in a handbasket.

134
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:28,000
But it didn't turn out to be true, did it?

135
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:30,000
If we think that way,

136
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:33,000
most of us end up at one or the other polar extreme.

137
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:36,000
We can conclude that we're not going to be able

138
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:38,000
to do anything about this

139
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,000
and we're just going to acquiesce to them,

140
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:43,000
And this kind of boys-will-be-boys attitude

141
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:46,000
is dangerous and wrong-headed

142
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:50,000
and because bad behavior is nothing to ignore,

143
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:54,000
So I'm not suggesting that we're going to ignore these things, okay.

144
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:57,000
On the other extreme, we can spend way too much time

145
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:02,000
and emotional capital fighting these outside influences.

146
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:06,000
If we focus a lot of aggression- and that's what it becomes-

147
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:09,000
on fighting outside influences,

148
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:13,000
that aggressiveness spills over into the way

149
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:16,000
that we work with our scouts and we start doing things

150
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:19,000
that send very clear messages to them that

151
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,000
you know we really don't like them very much

152
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:26,000
And we kind of draw a line between ourselves and them

153
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:29,000
And what we end up with is not what we want to have,

154
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:33,000
which is a partnership together with them in development.

155
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:38,000
We end up with an us and them kind of mentality.

156
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:42,000
I mean, if you start a direct attack on stubbornness

157
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:45,000
and laziness and indifference and short-sightedness

158
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
and foolishness, you're taking on society

159
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,000
and you're going to lose.

160
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,000
You're just going to lose in the end.

161
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:55,000
BF Skinner in Walden II- if you've ever read the book,

162
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
said this: each of us has interests

163
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:00,000
which conflict with the interests of everybody else.

164
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:03,000
Everybody else we call society

165
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:06,000
It's a powerful opponent and it always wins.

166
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:08,000
Society wins in the long run

167
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:12,000
because it has the advantage of numbers and age.

168
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:16,000
So if we're not going to fight society,

169
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:19,000
if we're not going to take a head-on assault

170
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:23,000
on all these difficult things that we see in scouts,

171
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:25,000
what are we supposed to do?

172
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:29,000
Well, what we're supposed to do is apply scouting,

173
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:36,000
and everything in scouting has a positive message behind it.

174
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:40,000
On my honor, I will do my best to do my duty to God

175
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:43,000
in my country and to obey the scout law,

176
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,000
to help other people at all times,

177
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
to keep myself physically strong, mentally awake

178
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
and morally straight. There's not a negative in there.

179
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:53,000
A scout is trustworthy, loyal, helpful, friendly, courteous,

180
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:57,000
kind, obedient, cheerful, thrifty, brave, clean and reverent.

181
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,000
There's no do-nots in there.

182
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:03,000
There's no prohibitions and there's no definitions.

183
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:08,000
The scout oath and law, we're making a promise to ourselves.

184
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:11,000
We're making an affirmative declaration

185
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:14,000
of the kind of person that we want to be

186
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:18,000
and we're aspiring to become that kind of person.

187
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:21,000
We aren't saying we're going to battle anything,

188
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:25,000
but that we are going to be something.

189
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:31,000
And with that promise and that law at its basis,

190
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:35,000
scouting becomes this marvelous, positive journey

191
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:39,000
about finding out what these things mean.

192
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:45,000
If we focus all that we do as scouters on those positive things-

193
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:47,000
and we all have them committed to memory,

194
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
so it should be pretty easy to do-

195
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:52,000
we don't have to battle with negative things-

196
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:55,000
And you've heard this before. right,

197
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:59,000
Instead of cursing the darkness, you turn on a light.

198
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:03,000
and it's an old, tired phrase, but it's true.

199
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:07,000
If we aim consciously and intentionally

200
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:12,000
at building an internal standard of what good is in our scouts,

201
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:15,000
we don't need to worry about bad,

202
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:18,000
because good is so much more powerful

203
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:19,000
than bad.

204
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:22,000
If we keep that in the forefront of what we do as scouters

205
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:25,000
and we keep these positive things in the forefront,

206
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:29,000
we won't have any time to fight with society.

207
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
Now, you and I as adults,

208
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:35,000
we've made some key connections in our brains and personalities.

209
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:39,000
I hope at least that scouts are still forming.

210
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
I think scouting is an ideal place

211
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
to form those connections

212
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
and to develop the character

213
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:48,000
to put everything that they learn in scouts

214
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:50,000
to use towards positive ends.

215
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
To me, that's the way scouting works.

216
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:56,000
It's this wonderful positive influence that you can champion

217
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:59,000
that will overcome any bad influence.

218
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
It may take time because, remember,

219
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:05,000
it's in cooperation with the developmental process

220
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:08,000
and as we develop and grow, we try out different things.

221
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:12,000
We try different attitudes and personalities,

222
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
and some of them are very positive

223
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
and some of them not so much.

224
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:19,000
But instead of getting exercised and excited

225
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,000
and fighting with those things that we see in scouts-

226
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:26,000
and my list was stubbornness, laziness, indifference,

227
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,000
shortsightedness and foolishness-

228
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:31,000
I think that pretty much covers it right-

229
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:34,000
If we stop fighting with those things

230
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,000
and we turn on the light,

231
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
those things start to disappear.

232
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:41,000
They're always going to be with us in one way or another.

233
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:44,000
We're going to see them in scouts and in ourselves.

234
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:50,000
So let's put our energy into what is really going to make a difference.

235
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:56,000
by focusing on every positive thing that we see in our scouts

236
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:59,000
and making sure that we call their attention to it,

237
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:03,000
and by constantly focusing on all the good,

238
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:07,000
all the wonderful potential in our scouts and in ourselves,

239
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:10,000
I think we're going to be happier that way, don't you?

240
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
I think our scouts will be happier that way

241
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,000
And we're going to actually do some good

242
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:16,000
and make the world a better place.

243
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
E-mail- that is folks.

244
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:46,000
And here's an answer to one of your e-mails.

245
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:58,000
I did answer a specific e-mail about this particular question,

246
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:01,000
but I get e-mails periodically

247
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:04,000
and I get a lot of e-mails.

248
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:06,000
I get a lot of e-mails.

249
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:08,000
I get a lot of e-mails-

250
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:13,000
E-mails periodically asking me to recommend

251
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:18,000
a junior leader or youth leader training curriculum

252
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:20,000
that a troop can present,

253
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:23,000
And my typical reply is as follows:

254
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:26,000
I've looked at dozens of different leadership training

255
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:30,000
schemes and syllabuses and curriculums and things like that.

256
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:32,000
I've even tried a few of them.

257
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:35,000
I've even tried writing a couple, but I don't use them,

258
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
And here's why

259
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:41,000
I think that scouts are interested in doing stuff.

260
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
They're not big picture people.

261
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
They want to do things,

262
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:48,000
not sit around and be told how things work

263
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:51,000
or the theory behind why we do them.

264
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
I can think of two examples

265
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:56,000
that help explain what I'm talking about.

266
00:14:56,000 --> 00:15:00,000
What happens if you're the coach of a team-

267
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,000
Let's call it a baseball team-

268
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:05,000
and a new player shows up?

269
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:08,000
Do we sit them down to a lecture on the theory of the game

270
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,000
and its history and its mechanics.

271
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:11,000
No,

272
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
After a quick introduction

273
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:16,000
we put them in the field and we throw them the ball.

274
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
We get them up and moving and active

275
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,000
and doing as a new player

276
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:22,000
and they're going to make a lot of mistakes

277
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:25,000
and they're not going to necessarily know what's going on.

278
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
but they will catch on

279
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:30,000
because they're actively involved in the game

280
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:33,000
And we have to remember that player came to play.

281
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
They came to play.

282
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
They don't want to sit on the bench and watch other people play.

283
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
Now they're going to practice a lot.

284
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
They're going to play a few minutes in a game here and there

285
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:44,000
and eventually they're going to begin to develop some skills.

286
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:47,000
But we're going to get them in the game.

287
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:51,000
Imagine if you try to teach someone to tie a square knot

288
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:54,000
using diagrams and presentations about the theory

289
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:57,000
and the history and the practice of knotting

290
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:00,000
and maybe a whole chapter about how rope is made.

291
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:03,000
They're going to grow kind of frustrated pretty quickly.

292
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
I would imagine I certainly would.

293
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:09,000
In comparison, if you take and you put the rope in their hands,

294
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
they're going to learn a whole lot faster

295
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
and it's a lot less frustrating for them

296
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
and maybe they'll become fascinated

297
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:18,000
with the idea of knots and ropes and things

298
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
and then want to know:

299
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
well, how did this rope get made

300
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
and what's the theory behind all this?

301
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,000
If a scout comes up to you and says:

302
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,000
I want to learn how to tie a square knot,

303
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
you put a piece of rope, you put it in their hands,

304
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
you break, tying the square knot

305
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
into its constituent elements- right,

306
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
Left over right, right over left-

307
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,000
and voila, there's a square knot.

308
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
They're going to practice,

309
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:41,000
They're going to try it out,

310
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:42,000
They're going to make a few mistakes.

311
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
They're going to tie a granny knot.

312
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
You're going to point out what the difference is,

313
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:49,000
but pretty soon they'll have attained the skill.

314
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:51,000
Now, I don't think training youth leaders

315
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:54,000
is any different than coaching a new player

316
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
or teaching somebody how to tie a square knot.

317
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:59,000
So you have a newly elected senior patrol leader,

318
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
newly elected patrol leader,

319
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:03,000
or the senior patrol leader is just appointed guys

320
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:06,000
into new positions of responsibility.

321
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
So where do we get started?

322
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
Well, my suggestion is

323
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
is that you train them in small steps,

324
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
just before they need the information

325
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
they're about to act on.

326
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
Our patrol leaders council meets to prepare

327
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
before every meeting,

328
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
And I may be able to take a minute or two

329
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
to ask questions about what they're going to do

330
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
and on occasion I'll spend a few minutes

331
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
telling them a few things

332
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
that might help them figure out

333
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
how to better do that.

334
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
And then we meet briefly afterwards

335
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
so that the senior patrol leader

336
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
can review what went on

337
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
and what their plans are for the next meeting,

338
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
And I get another opportunity to ask questions

339
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
and perhaps share information.

340
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:47,000
When I train youth leaders in this way,

341
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
in these relatively small steps,

342
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:52,000
I can relate fairly abstract concepts

343
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:55,000
to what they're actually doing right now,

344
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:58,000
rather than having them sit through some long event

345
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:01,000
where they're going to hear me talk for a long time.

346
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:04,000
The point is is that they're actually in the game.

347
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
They have their hands on the proverbial

348
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,000
rope right.

349
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
They're doing things and learning

350
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
as they go along.

351
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
Youth leaders need to know

352
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
what to do in the next few minutes

353
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,000
And what we do as scouters is give them

354
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
what they need and then

355
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:23,000
kind of stitch it together for them in review

356
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,000
Pretty soon. they're going to be self-starting.

357
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:27,000
They're going to pick up the ideas and put them to work.

358
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:29,000
Back to my baseball analogy.

359
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:31,000
Here's a glove. You put it on this hand,

360
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,000
Step over there 10 feet.

361
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,000
I'm going to throw the ball to you catch it.

362
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
Okay, you're going to need to use both hands.

363
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
You can't just catch it in the gloved hand

364
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:42,000
all by itself. You catch it and you trap it.

365
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
You got that. Now let's try that out for a little while.

366
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
Back up a little bit more.

367
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
Do you see what I mean? Active,

368
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
I'm doing it. I'm actually working

369
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
in the field.

370
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
I'm not getting a theoretical discourse

371
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
about how this is supposed to happen.

372
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
Now, this is a little counterintuitive

373
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
to the way that we think training

374
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
youth leaders should work.

375
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
What we want to do is we want

376
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
to sit down and explain

377
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
every possible thing that we can

378
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
about leadership and about planning

379
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
and preparation

380
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
and how to make things happen.

381
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
We want to give them the benefit

382
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
of our experience and knowledge.

383
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
We figure that the best way to do this

384
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
is to do it in some kind of training

385
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
event,

386
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
Something that we especially set aside.

387
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
Then, in this kind of classroom

388
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
atmosphere,

389
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
we're going to give them all of this information.

390
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
They're going to dutifully note it

391
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
or write it down, or practice it

392
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
or whatever. Then they'll

393
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
be able to go out and be leaders.

394
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
Basically,

395
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
hey, we've trained them

396
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
and now they're leaders.

397
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
They should be able to do it.

398
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
Anybody who's tried it knows

399
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
that it doesn't work that way.

400
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
No matter what kind of a course

401
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
you put together, no matter what kind

402
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
of an event you put together,

403
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
you're still going to be in the position

404
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
that I've just described

405
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
of coaching and mentoring

406
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
and moving along.

407
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
My tendency is to

408
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
skip over the first part.

409
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
Let's not worry about having a big event

410
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
or having this long training

411
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
thing. These guys are pretty sharp.

412
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
They're very action oriented.

413
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
They're not really interested in theory

414
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
to begin with. What they want to do

415
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
is make things happen. So let's get them making

416
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
things happen. Let them make the mistake

417
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
and try the shortcuts

418
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
and do all the stuff that we know

419
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
is going to happen anyway,

420
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
whether we have a training event or not.

421
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
Let them go through that and be right

422
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
there to work with and to explain

423
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
to them what's going to happen,

424
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
Along with getting requests for

425
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
training curriculums and things like that.

426
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
a lot of times, those requests

427
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
are attached to the thought that

428
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
we really want to get the patrol

429
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
method going in our troop.

430
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
We really want to start getting the patrol

431
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
system going and we need to have a starting

432
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
point and we think that training

433
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
everybody is going to be a good idea first.

434
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
Well, yes and no,

435
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
I don't want to say that

436
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
I would absolutely

437
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
reject the idea of having a sit down

438
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
and a talk about this, but I don't think

439
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
it needs to be all day long, I don't think it needs

440
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
to be an hour. I think what you do

441
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
is you sit down with the patrol leaders council

442
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
and say, hey, we are running

443
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
on something called the patrol system

444
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
as of now. It's going to be different

445
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
than what we've done before

446
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
and it's going to take us all

447
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
a little bit of time to understand how it works,

448
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,000
but it's going to start now. I'm going to

449
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
make just as many mistakes as you will,

450
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,000
but let's go for it

451
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
And then you start moving ahead. If the patrol

452
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,000
system is something that's new to the Scouts

453
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
and new to the troop,

454
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
it's definitely going to be new to you

455
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
and you're going to be learning as you

456
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
go along. but there's no way

457
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
to use a training

458
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
event or something like that to

459
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
inoculate ourselves against the

460
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
learning process that needs

461
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
to happen in actual

462
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
time as Scouts are trying

463
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
things out. I got to tell you

464
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
from my standpoint: it's a whole lot more fun.

465
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
It's never very orderly

466
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
and it's not all that easy,

467
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
but it certainly is

468
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
a whole lot more fun. Now, if you're running

469
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
training events and you're getting good

470
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
results from them, don't let me stop you.

471
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
I'm just sharing what

472
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
works for me and what I've found to be

473
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
effective. If you've got a question about

474
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,000
scouting that you are looking for an answer

475
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
to, you can get in touch with me

476
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
and I'm going to tell you how to do that in just

477
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
a moment.

